WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.466
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Cambridge exams.

2
00:00:05.766 --> 00:00:09.350
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.650 --> 00:00:18.258
Unit seven: B1 Preliminary: Gapped Text and Cloze. Today you will use grammar and context to complete missing sentences and words.

4
00:00:18.558 --> 00:00:21.187
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.133 --> 00:00:25.180
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.017 --> 00:00:27.053
Gapped text.

7
00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.990
Gapped text means a text with sentences removed.

8
00:00:31.583 --> 00:00:33.432
Part 4 is a gapped text.

9
00:00:34.017 --> 00:00:37.064
Your turn. Say: Gapped text.

10
00:00:39.900 --> 00:00:41.342
Gapped text.

11
00:00:42.083 --> 00:00:43.177
Sentence.

12
00:00:44.183 --> 00:00:47.228
Sentence means a group of words with a subject and verb.

13
00:00:47.817 --> 00:00:49.491
Choose the correct sentence.

14
00:00:50.083 --> 00:00:52.712
Your turn. Say: Sentence.

15
00:00:55.567 --> 00:00:56.730
Sentence.

16
00:00:57.483 --> 00:00:58.299
Pronoun.

17
00:00:59.283 --> 00:01:01.759
Pronoun means a word like it, they or this.

18
00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.013
The pronoun refers back.

19
00:01:04.617 --> 00:01:07.153
Your turn. Say: Pronoun.

20
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:10.850
Pronoun.

21
00:01:11.617 --> 00:01:12.699
Linking word.

22
00:01:13.683 --> 00:01:16.414
Linking word means a word that connects ideas.

23
00:01:17.017 --> 00:01:18.680
However is a linking word.

24
00:01:19.283 --> 00:01:22.063
Your turn. Say: Linking word.

25
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.040
Linking word.

26
00:01:26.783 --> 00:01:27.993
Open cloze.

27
00:01:28.983 --> 00:01:32.167
Open cloze means a text where you write each missing word.

28
00:01:32.783 --> 00:01:34.748
Part 6 is an open cloze.

29
00:01:35.350 --> 00:01:38.281
Your turn. Say: Open cloze.

30
00:01:41.133 --> 00:01:42.390
Open cloze.

31
00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:44.209
Preposition.

32
00:01:45.217 --> 00:01:47.855
Preposition means a word like in, on or at.

33
00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:50.415
Choose the correct preposition.

34
00:01:51.017 --> 00:01:54.122
Your turn. Say: Preposition.

35
00:01:56.967 --> 00:01:57.956
Preposition.

36
00:01:58.717 --> 00:01:59.602
Article.

37
00:02:00.617 --> 00:02:02.848
Article means a, an or the.

38
00:02:03.450 --> 00:02:04.985
Use an article here.

39
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:07.864
Your turn. Say: Article.

40
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.562
Article.

41
00:02:12.317 --> 00:02:13.863
Auxiliary verb.

42
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:17.883
Auxiliary verb means a helping verb like have or do.

43
00:02:18.483 --> 00:02:20.193
Add an auxiliary verb.

44
00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:23.807
Your turn. Say: Auxiliary verb.

45
00:02:26.667 --> 00:02:28.492
Auxiliary verb.

46
00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.113
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.100
Visal, how do I do a gapped text?

48
00:02:37.217 --> 00:02:48.228
Read the text and the missing sentences. For each gap, look at the sentence before and after it for pronouns, linking words and repeated ideas that show the connection.

49
00:02:49.317 --> 00:02:50.991
What about the open cloze?

50
00:02:52.083 --> 00:03:01.562
You write one word for each gap. Most answers are small grammar words such as prepositions, articles, auxiliaries, pronouns and linking words.

51
00:03:02.650 --> 00:03:03.698
How do I check?

52
00:03:04.783 --> 00:03:15.368
Read the sentence with your word in it. It must be grammatical and make sense. If you are not sure, think of the usual phrase, such as interested in or look forward to.

53
00:03:16.533 --> 00:03:18.521
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:19.383 --> 00:03:22.799
Grammar focus: Strategy: read around the gap.

55
00:03:23.099 --> 00:03:24.263
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:29.155
The gap needs a preposition: interested in, not interested of.

57
00:03:30.050 --> 00:03:33.501
The pronoun 'it' shows that the sentence refers to something earlier.

58
00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:42.332
For gapped texts, find links between the sentences. For an open cloze, think of grammar words and read the sentence back to test your answer.

59
00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:46.595
Be careful! Don't say: I write interested of.

60
00:03:48.533 --> 00:03:50.544
Say: I write interested in.

61
00:03:50.844 --> 00:03:52.484
Learn fixed phrases.

62
00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:55.848
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:56.633 --> 00:03:57.913
Grammar Words.

64
00:03:58.213 --> 00:04:00.851
Dara looks at an open cloze about a school trip.

65
00:04:01.867 --> 00:04:04.470
The first gap comes after the word interested.

66
00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:07.931
She knows that the correct preposition is in.

67
00:04:08.933 --> 00:04:11.955
The second gap needs a helping verb after the word has.

68
00:04:12.967 --> 00:04:15.303
She reads each sentence again to check it.

69
00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:20.402
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:21.250 --> 00:04:23.946
In an open cloze you write blank word per gap.

71
00:04:24.246 --> 00:04:27.110
A, no. B, one. C, five.

72
00:04:30.667 --> 00:04:32.260
The answer is B: one.

73
00:04:33.417 --> 00:04:35.393
Gaps are often blank words.

74
00:04:35.693 --> 00:04:39.126
A, very rare. B, long. C, grammar.

75
00:04:42.667 --> 00:04:44.631
The answer is C: grammar.

76
00:04:45.783 --> 00:04:47.040
Which is correct?

77
00:04:47.340 --> 00:04:52.270
A, interested in. B, interested of. C, interested at.

78
00:04:55.833 --> 00:04:57.798
The answer is A: interested in.

79
00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.764
A pronoun can show blank.

80
00:05:01.064 --> 00:05:05.507
A, a price. B, a link to earlier text. C, a date.

81
00:05:09.067 --> 00:05:12.471
The answer is B: a link to earlier text.

82
00:05:13.617 --> 00:05:15.187
After writing, you should blank.

83
00:05:15.487 --> 00:05:20.440
A, skip it. B, erase everything. C, read the sentence back.

84
00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:26.952
The answer is C: read the sentence back.

85
00:05:28.167 --> 00:05:31.214
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:32.050 --> 00:05:34.270
How do you complete an open cloze?

87
00:05:43.083 --> 00:05:44.700
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:59.880
I read the whole text first. For each gap I think about which grammar word is missing, such as a preposition, an article or a pronoun. Then I read the sentence again to check that it makes sense.

89
00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:01.982
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:02.883 --> 00:06:07.834
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: read around the gap.

91
00:06:08.134 --> 00:06:12.853
Today's value is logical rigor. Follow your reasoning wherever it honestly leads.

92
00:06:13.153 --> 00:06:15.469
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

93
00:06:15.769 --> 00:06:18.305
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

